@page "/exgrid/appearance"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Appearance"
          Lead="Geometry travels as C# parameters, because the grid's arithmetic depends on it. Colour travels as CSS custom properties, because your theme owns it. Keeping the two apart is what lets a theme restyle the grid without knocking its rows out of line.">

    <DocsSection Title="Density and row height">
        <p>
            <code>Density</code> picks a consistent set of row height, header height, type size and padding:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>Compact</code> — the default, 28&nbsp;px rows.</li>
            <li><code>Standard</code> — 32&nbsp;px rows, the height of MudBlazor's tables.</li>
            <li><code>Comfortable</code> — 40&nbsp;px rows and wider padding.</li>
            <li><code>Excel</code> — Excel's default 20&nbsp;px row, at a smaller type.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>RowHeight</code> and <code>HeaderHeight</code> override the preset, value by value.
            <strong>Change row height through the parameter, never in CSS.</strong> Virtualisation, the selection
            overlay and the editor's position are all computed from it; a height set only in a stylesheet paints
            rows that the arithmetic no longer matches, slightly at first and more the further you scroll.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>StripeRows</code> paints every second row, counted from the start of the whole result, so a stripe
            stays with its row as you scroll. <code>HighlightFocusRow</code> and <code>HighlightHoverRow</code> are
            covered under <a href="exgrid/selection">Selection</a>.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Density, stripes and the Focus row" Builtin="typeof(DensityGrid)" Mud="typeof(DensityGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Visual Tokens">
        <p>
            Every colour the grid paints is a CSS custom property with an <code>--ex-</code> prefix, read from the
            nearest element that sets it. Set them on a wrapper, a page section or your theme's root; the grid itself
            takes no colour parameter and no <code>Class</code>. Among them:
        </p>
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 42%">Token</th><th>Paints</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-background</code>, <code>--ex-color</code></td><td>The grid's ground and text.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-pinned-background</code></td><td>The ground of pinned cells, which must be opaque.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-header-background</code>, <code>--ex-header-color</code>, <code>--ex-header-rule-color</code></td><td>The header band.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-row-rule-color</code>, <code>--ex-column-rule-color</code></td><td>Lines between rows and columns; transparent until set.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-row-stripe-background</code></td><td>Row Stripes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-selection-fill</code>, <code>--ex-selection-outline</code>, <code>--ex-focus-outline</code>, <code>--ex-focus-among-ranges-outline</code></td><td>The selection and the Focus cell. The last is the Focus's thin border among several ranges, and is the Focus outline's colour unless you set it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-heading-mark</code></td><td>The Heading Mark's line, marked letters and all-selected corner triangle. The Focus outline's colour unless you set it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-heading-mark-background</code>, <code>--ex-heading-mark-whole-background</code></td><td>The marked Heading's ground: the first for part of a column or row, the second when every cell in it is selected.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-focus-row-fill</code>, <code>--ex-row-hover-background</code></td><td>The Focus row and hover bands.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-tone-positive-color</code>, <code>--ex-tone-negative-color</code></td><td>Tones. Unset, a toned cell looks like any other.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-state-stale-color</code>, <code>--ex-state-error-color</code>, <code>--ex-state-missing-background</code>, <code>--ex-state-modified-mark</code></td><td>Cell States. Each is a colour: the modified mark's is the corner triangle's.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-change-highlight-background</code></td><td>The Change Highlight on live data.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-match-mark-fill</code>, <code>--ex-match-mark-current-fill</code></td><td>The matches on screen while the find panel holds a search, and the current match: by default a light and a strong tint of one orange (<code>#ff9632</code> at 25% and 55%), apart from the Change Highlight's yellow.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--ex-pasting-bar</code>, <code>--ex-pasting-bar-height</code>, <code>--ex-pasting-cursor</code></td><td>A paste under way: the bar across the grid's top edge (<code>Highlight</code>, 3px) and the pointer over it (<code>progress</code>), from the paste's start until it lands or is refused. See Clipboard. They paint work you run through <code>CancellableInOrderAsync</code> the same way, such as ExSheet opening a file.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            The selection's shade is set by <code>--ex-selection-fill</code> alone. It is painted by an element of
            the grid's own, beneath each selected range's box, so a rule on <code>ex-range</code>, the box that draws
            the outline, or on anything inside it, does not reach the shade.
        </p>
        <p>
            A single range is outlined in <code>--ex-selection-outline</code>, and the Focus inside it is only left
            unshaded. Among several ranges, made with Ctrl+click, no range carries that outline and no fill handle is
            shown, and the Focus cell takes a thin border of one device pixel inside it, where Excel's active cell was
            observed to have one, in <code>--ex-focus-among-ranges-outline</code>. Unset, that is the Focus outline's
            colour, so a theme that colours <code>--ex-focus-outline</code> colours this border too, and under
            ExGrid.MudBlazor it is the palette's primary. The theme in "A theme, tones and a stale price" below sets
            <code>--ex-focus-outline</code> and not <code>--ex-focus-among-ranges-outline</code>: Ctrl+click in it
            (built-in).
        </p>
        <p>
            The <a href="exgrid/selection#rectangles--a-focus-and-an-extent">Heading Mark</a> shades each header and
            Row Heading whose column or row holds part of the selection, colours its letters, and draws a line two
            device pixels thick on its edge toward the cells. A whole column or row takes a distinct shade. Where
            the grid has a Headings corner, its triangle takes the mark's colour when every cell is selected.
            <code>--ex-heading-mark</code> colours the line, letters and triangle, using the Focus outline's colour
            unless you set it. The two background tokens above set the shades. A bare grid uses system colours;
            ExGrid.MudBlazor uses its palette, with the primary for the line and letters. Your Theme's tokens win,
            and the Headings follow the frame's colour scheme. Under forced colours the shades are dropped and
            the line and marked letters use the system's highlight colour.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where a header or Row Heading covers a side of the Focus or a single range, the Heading Mark supplies
            that side's two device pixels; there is no second outline beside it. At a pinned block's edge, or a
            grid edge without a Heading, the outline stays inside the cell. The thin Focus border among several
            ranges is unchanged.
            The ordinary Focus and single-range outline defaults to two device pixels on every side,
            including at display scales above 100%.
        </p>
        <p>
            The font family is the exception. The grid decides whether a number fits — or becomes <code>####</code> —
            from the width of its glyphs, so <code>--ex-font-family</code> is a metric as much as a look: whoever
            changes it also passes matching <code>CellMetrics</code>. <code>MudExGridPaper</code> does this for
            Roboto. In ExGrid for React the grid takes the same two: <code>cellMetrics</code>, a
            <code>CellTextMetrics</code> of the widths, and <code>presentationDefaults</code>, a
            <code>GridPresentationDefaults</code> a design-system Wrapper hands every grid inside it with its font's
            widths and the Density, hover band and Row Stripes its own words map onto. A value given to the grid beats
            the Wrapper's, and the Wrapper's beats the preset.
        </p>
        <p>
            For a MudBlazor theme in another font, give the paper a <code>MudExGridFont</code> as its
            <code>Font</code>: the family and the widths measured for it, in one value. The paper writes the family
            as <code>--ex-font-family</code> and hands the grid the widths in the same render, so the font on screen
            and the widths behind <code>####</code> cannot come from different hands. It does not read the theme's
            typography, because a font the grid was not measured for would put one font on screen and another's
            widths in the arithmetic. Below, the theme's font is the system font, the one ExGrid's own stylesheet
            paints, so its widths are the ones ExGrid measured: under the built-in Chrome there is nothing to set.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A font other than Roboto" Builtin="typeof(FontGrid)" Mud="typeof(FontGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])" />
        <p>
            Those metrics are a <code>CellTextMetrics</code>: a width per class of character, and a table of single
            glyphs measured on their own. When something that is not the grid sizes text that the grid will show —
            a server that computes a report and decides its label widths, as ExPivot's server source does — hand it
            the same numbers. <code>CellTextMetrics.ExportGlyphWidths()</code> copies the glyph table as plain widths,
            already scaled to these metrics' font size and weight, so the other process charges each glyph exactly
            what the grid charges it. The character classes still come first there, as they do in the grid; metrics
            with no table export an empty one.
        </p>
        <h3 class="mud-typography mud-typography-h6 docs-example-title">Cell States</h3>
        <p>
            A tone is derived from the value. A <strong>Cell State</strong> is what only your application knows
            about a cell — <code>Stale</code>, <code>Missing</code>, <code>Error</code> or <code>Modified</code> —
            asked for through the <code>CellState</code> delegate, once per painted cell. Keep it in a field and keep
            it to a lookup; hand over a new delegate when the answers change.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A theme, tones and a stale price" Builtin="typeof(ThemedGrid)" Mud="typeof(ThemedGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Dark scheme">
        <p>
            Untouched, the built-in grid paints with the system colours <code>Canvas</code> and <code>CanvasText</code>,
            so it follows the page's <code>color-scheme</code> and the user's forced-colours setting with no work. Declare
            <code>color-scheme: dark</code> on your page and the grid is dark. Tokens you set can follow too, with
            <code>light-dark()</code>, as the theme above does. Inside a <code>MudExGridPaper</code>, the tokens come
            from MudBlazor's palette, so the grid follows <code>MudThemeProvider</code>'s dark mode.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Built-in Chrome or MudBlazor's">
        <p>
            The column menu, the filter panel, the find panel, the Context Menu, the Cell Editor and the loading
            indicator are the grid's <strong>Chrome</strong>, drawn through <code>IGridChrome</code>. With no
            <code>Chrome</code> parameter you get the built-in one, which needs nothing but ExGrid's own stylesheet,
            <code>&lt;ExGridStylesheet /&gt;</code>. ExGrid.MudBlazor adds:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>MudGridChrome</code> — the same seams drawn with MudBlazor's own controls. Pass it as <code>Chrome</code>.</li>
            <li><code>MudExGridPaper</code> — a Material surface around the grid that maps <code>Dense</code>, <code>Hover</code> and <code>Striped</code> onto the grid and sets Roboto with its measured widths.</li>
            <li><code>mud-ex-grid.css</code>, placed with <code>&lt;MudExGridStylesheet /&gt;</code> — the Visual Tokens mapped onto MudBlazor's palette, dark mode included.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A Chrome declares the stylesheets it brings through <code>Stylesheets</code>, and the grid shows a notice
            for each when the page holds another version of it, as it does for its own
            (<a href="getting-started#when-a-page-holds-another-version-s-files">When a page holds another version's files</a>).
            <code>MudGridChrome</code> declares <code>mud-ex-grid.css</code>; the built-in Chrome declares none.
        </p>
        <p>
            Chrome draws and calls back; the grid decides what every choice means. The same filter chosen under
            either Chrome gives the same rows. Every Example on this site runs under both: switch with
            <em>Built-in / MudBlazor</em> at the top of the page.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A Chrome of your own</strong> implements <code>IGridChrome</code>, and two of its duties are
            easy to miss. Each popover's context — the column menu's, the Context Menu's, the filter panel's and
            the find panel's — hands it a <code>TakeFocus</code> function: the contents take the keyboard by
            calling it with their control, never with the control's own <code>FocusAsync</code>. Over Blazor
            Server the request lands a round trip after the key that opened the popover, and the grid gives the
            control the keyboard only while it is still the grid's, so a user who has pressed another grid in
            the meantime keeps it there. And <code>IGridChrome.Words</code> answers the words a refusal is
            announced in, by the ids in <code>RefusalLabelIds</code>; answering <code>null</code> leaves the
            built-in English (<a href="exgrid/clipboard#what-a-refusal-sounds-like">what a refusal sounds like</a>).
            A third: a button that closes its panel — OK, Apply, Cancel — carries the class
            <code>ex-popover-closes</code>, as the built-in and MudBlazor panels' do, so that the keys typed while
            the panel goes wait for the keyboard to land rather than reach a button that is going.
            A fourth: a control of your Chrome whose event should keep its place among the keys typed around it
            carries <code>data-ex-claims</code>, naming the events it claims — <code>click</code>,
            <code>keydown</code>, <code>submit</code> — as the built-in and MudBlazor ones do. Its handler then calls
            the grid through the context's delegates every time that event comes: a menu resolves the key through
            <code>ResolveKey</code>, and a filter form hands each submission to <code>Apply</code>, one that cannot
            be applied as the answer <code>Apply</code> refuses (an <code>In</code> with no values). A find panel
            hands its steps to <code>FindContext.Next</code> and <code>Previous</code>, and each tick of its two
            options to <code>MatchCaseChanged</code> and <code>WholeCellChanged</code>, which return a
            <code>Task</code> the handler returns in turn; the claim goes on the checkbox itself
            (a <code>MudCheckBox</code> passes it there), not on a label around it, whose click the browser
            passes on to the checkbox as a second one. A claimed
            event that never reaches the grid leaves the keys after it waiting for the next one.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>In ExGrid for React</strong> (<code>@@ex-grid/react</code>, published with 0.3.0) the same seams are
            the <code>chrome</code> prop, one render function per part, each handed what the grid hands that part. The
            column menu (<code>columnMenu</code>) and the Context Menu (<code>contextMenu</code>) are the first two; a
            part not given is the built-in Chrome's. A menu draws one item per entry of <code>context.commands</code>,
            in that order, and spreads <code>useMenuItems(context)(index)</code> onto each item's control: those props
            hand every key on it to the grid, which answers with the move, the run or the close its table means, run a
            command as the built-in item does, and put the keyboard on the first enabled item whenever the grid asks.
            The words are <code>context.label(id)</code>, your <code>commandLabel</code> or the built-in English. What
            you render is a component of its own, kept with its state while its menu stands.
        </p>
        <p>
            A column's filter panel (<code>filterPanel</code>) stands under the column menu's commands. It draws
            <code>useFilterPanel(context).view</code>, the value list or the condition form as the grid holds it,
            and hands each choice to its setter on the context (<code>setChecked</code>, <code>setSearch</code>,
            <code>setOperator</code>, …): what a choice means, what OK applies (<code>context.apply()</code>) and why
            a typed value does not read are the grid's, so the same choices give the same rows under any Chrome. The
            hook's <code>first</code>, <code>last</code> and <code>search</code> refs name the controls Tab, Shift+Tab
            and E on a command put the keyboard on, and <code>onValueListKeyDown</code> on the value list lets
            Excel's letters run their command there too. The part is a form the grid made: Enter in a text field
            submits it as the browser does, and that applies as OK does, so draw no form of your own inside it.
            The words are <code>context.label(id)</code>, by the ids of <code>filterPanelLabelIds</code>, and an
            operator's name by <code>operatorLabelId(operator)</code>; the Blank's words in the view and why a typed
            value does not read come resolved the same way.
        </p>
        <p>
            The find panel (<code>findPanel</code>) draws <code>useFindPanel(context).view</code>: the text, the two
            options, whether Replace is shown and whether a Replace All is still out, and what the last step came to,
            in the grid's words, for a live region. Each change and step goes to the context (<code>setText</code>,
            <code>setMatchCase</code>, <code>next</code>, <code>replaceAll</code>, …); the search, its answer and the
            Focus it moves stay the grid's. Hand the hook's <code>field</code> ref to the find field, which takes the
            keyboard with its text selected when Find opens, and as it was after a Tab off either end of the panel. Enter and Shift+Enter in a field step through the
            grid's own form; a button of yours that submits it steps nothing, so have each button call the step it
            means. The words are <code>context.label(id)</code>, by the ids of <code>findPanelLabelIds</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Cell Editor (<code>cellEditor</code>) draws one text field in the grid's editor box over the cell, from
            <code>useCellEditor(context)</code>: hand it the hook's <code>field</code> ref and <code>defaultValue</code>,
            and no <code>value</code>. The text is the grid's: it reads the field at the Enter that commits it and writes
            it where it types itself, as from the Formula Bar, so the field stays uncontrolled. The field takes the
            keyboard once drawn, with the caret at its end; Enter, Tab, Escape, F2 and Overwrite's arrows stay the
            grid's, and the hook's <code>view</code> says the mode (<code>point</code> among them, where Formula entry
            points), a Reject's sentence and a pending commit, which the grid has already marked on the field. The
            colours of a Formula's References are laid over the grid's own field only, so a field drawn here shows its
            text plain, and the Formula Bar still colours it. The box carries the cell's padding, outline and background, so style
            the field to fill it with none of its own.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Formula Bar's two fields are slots too. <code>formulaBarText</code> draws one text field in the bar's
            box, from <code>useFormulaBarText(context)</code>: hand it the hook's <code>field</code> ref, and no
            <code>value</code>. The grid writes the Focus cell's text into it, names it and makes it read only where the
            cell does not edit; a press into it opens the edit there, and what is typed is the Cell Editor's, committed
            once at Enter. The hook's <code>view</code> says whether the field is read only and whether its text is
            borrowed, an array's Formula over a cell it spills into, which you show dimmed. <code>nameBox</code> draws
            the Name Box's field from <code>useNameBox(context)</code>: the grid writes the Focus's name into it,
            selects it when a press takes the box, and hands what Enter entered to <code>onNameBoxEntered</code>, through
            a form of its own, so draw no form and no key handler. Neither field takes the keyboard by itself. Hold
            your <code>chrome</code> in a constant or a memo: another render function draws the bar's fields again,
            once the keyboard has left them.
        </p>
        <p>
            Three slots only show what the grid decides. <code>cellMessage</code> draws a cell's message in the grid's
            tooltip below the cell: <code>context.message</code> is the Consumer's sentence or the Reject's, and
            <code>editorError</code> says which. <code>selectionSummary</code> draws <code>context.figures</code> in the
            status line, each with the id of its name and its text as the grid formatted it, and the pending words or
            the decline's reason while there are none; the right-click that opens the figures menu stays the grid's.
            <code>loadingIndicator</code> stands at the grid's foot and is handed whether rows are on their way and how
            many painted rows are Placeholders; it draws nothing while nothing loads, and the Placeholder rows stay the
            grid's. Each is drawn again when what it shows changes.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Words in another language.</strong> Every word the built-in Chrome shows in ExGrid for React — a
            command, a panel's buttons and list, an operator, a refusal, the Mark Column, the status line — has an id,
            and the grid asks your <code>commandLabel</code> for it first, keeping the built-in English where you answer
            <code>undefined</code>. <code>builtInLabelIds()</code> lists the ids. Japanese for all of them, Excel's where Excel has the word, ships
            as <code>japaneseGridWords</code>: hand it as <code>commandLabel=&#123;japaneseGridWords&#125;</code>, or answer
            your own words first, <code>id =&gt; mine(id) ?? japaneseGridWords(id)</code>. The words never follow the
            <code>locale</code>, which reads and writes values only: a Japanese reader of a ledger kept in
            <code>en-US</code> chooses the words, and the grid does not guess them.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Your own control in the grid's order.</strong> A handler that changes what the keys after it act on —
            a button of your Chrome, a control in your popover, a command beside the grid — can run in its place among
            the keys and presses through <code>InOrderAsync(kind, handling)</code> on the grid, or the
            <code>InOrder</code> delegate on a popover's <code>GridPopoverContext</code>, with the
            <code>GridInputKind</code> of the event it answers. Marked with <code>data-ex-claims</code>, a control inside
            the grid's markup — your popover's contents in the grid's frame, your Chrome's controls — has its event
            numbered as it happens, and <code>handling</code> runs after every input made before it and before every one
            made after it; one with no mark, or outside the grid, runs after every input the grid has received so far.
            <code>KeyboardIn</code> and <code>KeyboardOut</code> are the exception: they run only when the grid tells
            the keyboard crossing the edge of a product's Input Root — the element marked
            <code>data-ex-input-root</code> around the grid, naming <code>focusin</code> and <code>focusout</code> in its
            own <code>data-ex-claims</code> — and a call anywhere else does nothing, since a focus change inside that
            element is no input and the grid cannot tell the two apart. ExSheet puts its toolbar, its Sheet Tabs,
            Format Cells and the keyboard crossing the Sheet's edge in its grid's order this way. A control of yours
            that a hand-off should reach without scrolling, such as the next tab of a tab list, takes the keyboard
            through <code>HandKeyboardToAsync(control, preventScroll: true)</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Work of your own in the grid's order.</strong> A command no control numbered — a button outside the
            grid, a service's call — takes a place of its own through <code>InOrderAsync(handling)</code>: after every
            input the grid has received, and the inputs made after it wait until it has finished. It never takes a
            number told for a control's event. Work that may take a while and that the user may cancel, such as reading a
            file, goes through <code>CancellableInOrderAsync(handling, cancelledTooLate)</code>. From the call until
            the work ends — waiting behind earlier inputs or running — the Settled Stamp names it
            (<code>data-ex-work-placed</code>), no longer says that an Escape leaves, and the Settled Mark is off; once
            its turn starts the root carries <code>aria-busy</code> and <code>data-ex-work</code> and is painted as a
            paste under way is. A plain <kbd>Escape</kbd> typed in the grid against a page whose stamp named the work
            cancels <code>handling</code>'s token and does nothing else: it closes no editor or popover, and does not
            release Tab. An Escape typed against a page whose stamp did not yet name it is an ordinary key, however
            soon it arrives: what it was typed against decides it, not when it comes. A second Escape finds the work
            cancelled, and is answered as one that came after it ended. Your work looks at the token until the point where it lands,
            and ends in <code>OperationCanceledException</code> once it is cancelled; past that point it lands
            synchronously whatever the token says. An Escape that came too late runs <code>cancelledTooLate(true)</code>
            in its own turn, and one that found the work already over otherwise <code>cancelledTooLate(false)</code>,
            for you to say so. The task answers whether the work ran to its end. Saying what the work did is yours: the
            grid says nothing of it. Both may be called from any thread; one cancellable work cannot run inside
            another, and disposing of the grid cancels the work under way. ExSheet opens a file this way
            (<a href="exsheet/xlsx">Open and save .xlsx</a>).
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A frame of your own</strong> outside the grid — a dialog, a toolbar — takes the keyboard through
            <code>HandKeyboardToFrameAsync</code> and gives it back through <code>ReturnKeyboardAsync</code>, with the
            frame's element and the keys it heard on the way. Await that return before removing your frame or
            clearing its queue: completion means the grid has collected those keys. A popover of yours in the grid's frame
            (<code>OpenPopoverAsync</code>) can ask to hear Escape close it, and hand the keyboard on from there. Its
            contents answer the context's <code>FocusRequest</code> and <code>FocusLastRequest</code> by handing the
            control to the context's <code>TakeFocus</code>, not with their own <code>FocusAsync</code>: a control the
            user presses as the popover appears keeps the keyboard, and the opening focus that lands after it is not
            taken. Over
            Blazor Server each of these lands a round trip late, and the grid holds the keys typed meanwhile and
            hands them on, in order, to where the keyboard lands. ExSheet's Sheet Toolbar is built this way. A dialog
            of yours with a grid inside, closing on the Escape that raises the grid's <code>OnLeave</code>, calls
            <code>PassKeysOutAsync</code> there: the keys typed after the Escape go out to your dialog, for it to hand
            on with the keyboard, as ExPivot's Show Details dialog does.
            A drawn control takes the held keyboard through <code>HandKeyboardToAsync</code>. A design-system
            control that exposes only its contents' container may pass that container: its first enabled text
            field takes the keyboard with its value selected, so held typing replaces the initial value.
            For a change-bound text field, an Enter, held or typed after the text is shown, or leaving the field commits the held text once.
            A control the user already chose keeps the keyboard. More Colours in ExSheet's MudBlazor Format Cells
            uses this to start in HEX.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A surface drawn outside the grid joins its order while it is open.</strong> A design system draws a
            dialog under its provider and a popover at the page's body, outside the element marked
            <code>data-ex-input-root</code>. <code>JoinSurfaceAsync(element, around, escape)</code> puts such a surface in
            the grid's order until you dispose of what it returns, or the surface leaves the page: the surface is
            <code>element</code>, or the nearest element around it that the CSS selector <code>around</code> matches — a
            <code>MudDialog</code>'s <code>[role="dialog"]</code>, which your content gets no reference to. Joined, its
            claimed clicks, presses, submissions and keys are numbered and handled in their place, the keyboard standing on
            it counts as the grid's, so a hand-on from it is granted, and every key typed in it while an earlier input is
            answered is held and handed on, in order, to where that input left the keyboard. A claim can name one key,
            <code>data-ex-claims="keydown:Enter keydown:Space"</code>, and on a joined surface it is told after the controls
            inside have heard the key, so a key a control keeps, Escape closing a dropdown's list, reaches no claim. An
            Escape no control claims or keeps is the surface's: the grid runs <code>escape</code> in that Escape's turn.
            Join before the surface takes the keyboard. A dialog that gives the keyboard back, as it closes, to whatever
            held it as it opened can have it wait first on an empty element of yours, marked
            <code>data-ex-claims=""</code>: <code>HandKeyboardToFrameAsync(element)</code> tells the hand-off and puts the
            keyboard there, and the keys typed on it go to the control that next takes the keyboard. ExSheet's Format
            Cells under the MudBlazor Chrome is built this way (<a href="exsheet/formatting#format-cells">Cell formatting</a>,
            the MudBlazor switch of its Example).
        </p>
